### What is the pair with target sum problem?

Given a sorted array and a target sum, find two elements that add up exactly to the target and return their indices. The brute-force approach checks every pair in O(n^2) time. On a sorted array the two-pointer technique solves it in O(n) time and O(1) space.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PairTargetSum {

    public static int[] findPair(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];
            if (sum == target) {
                return new int[]{left, right};
            } else if (sum > target) {
                right--;
            } else {
                left++;
            }
        }
        return new int[]{-1, -1};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] settlements = {150, 300, 450, 700, 950};
        int target = 750;
        int[] result = findPair(settlements, target);
        System.out.println("Indices: [" + result[0] + ", " + result[1] + "]");
        System.out.println("Values: " + settlements[result[0]] + " + "
                + settlements[result[1]] + " = " + target);
    }
}
```

```text
Indices: [1, 2]
Values: 300 + 450 = 750
```

### Why does the two-pointer technique work?

The correctness relies on the sorted order. When `arr[left] + arr[right]` is too large, pairing `arr[right]` with anything to the left of the current `left` would be even smaller, but pairing it with anything to the right of the current `left` would not help because we already know the sum is too big with the current largest right. So `right` can never be part of the answer with this or any larger left, and we safely discard it. The same reasoning applies symmetrically when the sum is too small.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TwoPointerReasoning {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {100, 200, 300, 400, 500};
        int target = 600;

        int left = 0, right = 4;
        // sum = 100 + 500 = 600 → found!
        System.out.println("left=" + left + " right=" + right
                + " sum=" + (arr[left] + arr[right]));

        // If sum were 700 (too big), right=4 (500) paired with anything
        // to the right of left would still be >= 600, so right is safely discarded
    }
}
```

```text
left=0 right=4 sum=600
```

### How is two-pointer different from a HashMap approach?

The HashMap approach stores each element's required complement and runs in O(n) time even on unsorted arrays, but uses O(n) space. The two-pointer approach uses O(1) space but requires the array to be sorted. If the data arrives already sorted, two-pointer is strictly better on space.

| Aspect | Two-pointer | HashMap |
|---|---|---|
| Precondition | Array sorted | None |
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space | O(1) | O(n) |
| Returns original indices? | Yes (if sorted in place) | Yes |
| Works on unsorted? | No | Yes |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class HashMapApproach {

    public static int[] findPairHashMap(int[] arr, int target) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            int complement = target - arr[i];
            if (seen.containsKey(complement)) {
                return new int[]{seen.get(complement), i};
            }
            seen.put(arr[i], i);
        }
        return new int[]{-1, -1};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] unsorted = {700, 150, 950, 300, 450};
        int target = 750;
        int[] result = findPairHashMap(unsorted, target);
        System.out.println("HashMap indices: [" + result[0] + ", " + result[1] + "]");
    }
}
```

```text
HashMap indices: [1, 3]
```

### What about returning one-based indices?

LeetCode Two Sum II returns one-based indices. Simply add one to each zero-based result.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class OneBasedIndices {

    public static int[] findPairOneBased(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];
            if (sum == target) return new int[]{left + 1, right + 1};
            else if (sum > target) right--;
            else left++;
        }
        return new int[]{-1, -1};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {150, 300, 450, 700, 950};
        int[] result = findPairOneBased(arr, 750);
        System.out.println("One-based: [" + result[0] + ", " + result[1] + "]");
    }
}
```

```text
One-based: [2, 3]
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include no valid pair, a two-element array, negative numbers, and duplicate values.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class PairEdgeCases {

    public static int[] findPair(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];
            if (sum == target) return new int[]{left, right};
            else if (sum > target) right--;
            else left++;
        }
        return new int[]{-1, -1};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("No pair: " + Arrays.toString(findPair(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, 10)));
        System.out.println("Two elem: " + Arrays.toString(findPair(new int[]{3, 7}, 10)));
        System.out.println("Negatives: " + Arrays.toString(findPair(new int[]{-5, -2, 1, 4, 8}, 2)));
        System.out.println("Duplicates: " + Arrays.toString(findPair(new int[]{3, 3, 4, 5}, 6)));
    }
}
```

```text
No pair: [-1, -1]
Two elem: [0, 1]
Negatives: [1, 3]
Duplicates: [0, 1]
```

### How does this generalize to three-sum and four-sum?

The pair-with-target-sum is the building block for three-sum and four-sum problems. For three-sum, fix one element and run the two-pointer technique on the rest. For four-sum, fix two elements and run the two-pointer technique.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class ThreeSum {

    public static List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] arr, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 2; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && arr[i] == arr[i - 1]) continue;
            int left = i + 1, right = arr.length - 1;
            while (left < right) {
                int sum = arr[i] + arr[left] + arr[right];
                if (sum == target) {
                    result.add(Arrays.asList(arr[i], arr[left], arr[right]));
                    while (left < right && arr[left] == arr[left + 1]) left++;
                    while (left < right && arr[right] == arr[right - 1]) right--;
                    left++;
                    right--;
                } else if (sum < target) {
                    left++;
                } else {
                    right--;
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {-500, -200, 0, 100, 200, 300, 500};
        System.out.println("Triplets summing to 0: " + threeSum(arr, 0));
    }
}
```

```text
Triplets summing to 0: [[-500, 0, 500], [-500, 200, 300], [-200, 0, 200]]
```

### What about using BigDecimal for financial amounts?

In banking applications, financial amounts must use `BigDecimal` to avoid floating-point rounding errors. The two-pointer logic is the same, but comparisons use `compareTo` instead of arithmetic operators.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PairBigDecimal {

    public static int[] findPair(BigDecimal[] arr, BigDecimal target) {
        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            BigDecimal sum = arr[left].add(arr[right]);
            int cmp = sum.compareTo(target);
            if (cmp == 0) return new int[]{left, right};
            else if (cmp > 0) right--;
            else left++;
        }
        return new int[]{-1, -1};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] settlements = {
                new BigDecimal("1500.50"),
                new BigDecimal("3000.75"),
                new BigDecimal("4500.25"),
                new BigDecimal("7000.00")
        };
        BigDecimal target = new BigDecimal("7501.00");
        int[] result = findPair(settlements, target);
        System.out.println("Pair: [" + result[0] + ", " + result[1] + "]");
        System.out.println("Values: " + settlements[result[0]] + " + "
                + settlements[result[1]] + " = " + target);
    }
}
```

```text
Pair: [1, 2]
Values: 3000.75 + 4500.25 = 7501.00
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know three things. First, the two-pointer method depends on the array being sorted; if it is not, sorting first costs O(n log n), and a hash-map approach might be better. Second, this technique generalizes to closest-sum, three-sum, and four-sum problems by nesting the two-pointer sweep inside one or two outer loops. Third, in production banking code, all monetary amounts should use `BigDecimal` to avoid floating-point rounding errors that could cause audit discrepancies.
